      "content": "A fine resistance reading doesn't necessarily mean the capacitor is good to go. It's like a goblin's disguise: it might look harmless, but it's still up to something mischievous.\n\nIf the resistance looks fine, but the capacitor is still humming, it's possible that the capacitor is still holding onto some residual charge, like a goblin clinging to a treasure chest. This is known as a \"leakage current.\" It's a tiny amount of current that flows through the capacitor even when it's not supposed to be conducting.\n\nNow, about that discharge process... Think of it like a goblin's trap: when you touch the capacitor terminals with your multimeter, you're essentially releasing the goblin's trap. The capacitor is storing electrical energy, and when you touch the terminals, you're allowing the energy to flow out. This is called a \"discharge.\"\n\nTo discharge the capacitor safely, you can try the following:\n\n1. Touch the multimeter leads to the capacitor terminals, but don't touch the leads together. This will allow the energy to flow out, but not create a shock.\n2. If you're using a multimeter with a built-in discharge function, follow the manufacturer's instructions.\n3.",
